@section ll-database.h 

In Lifelines, record keys were always of the form

@code{letter one-or-more-digits}

with the @code{letter} being one of the five letters @code{I,F,S,E,X}
for person, family, source, event, and other records, respectively.

In a DeadEnds library, there is no such restriction.  However, there
are parts of Lifelines, including the port of it to DeadEnds, that
still assume that keys have this format.

In Lifelines, @code{KEYNUM_TYPE} was sometimes 32-bits and sometimes
64-bits -- depending on the platform.  In DeadEnds, support for
64-bits in personal systems has become sufficiently ubiquitous, that
keynums are always 64-bits.  And the maximum key number
(@code{MAXKEYNUMBER})is currently 999,999,999,999.  As this only
requires 40 bits for storage, it could be raised, without much pain, by
a factor of a thousand or even a million, but I see no need to do so.

If you change @code{MAXKEYNUMBER}, then you should adjust
@code{MAXKEYWIDTH} as well.

